From 0d043aa3132b48dffaad0785e17ee9f792c9198d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 1aeo <github@1aeo.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 08:56:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 11/15] dos: Add off-by-default HSDir circuit-reuse and
 previous-hop defenses

Tor and Arti clients and onion services make each onion service
descriptor fetch or upload on a fresh circuit through relays from the
consensus. Stock software doesn't break either pattern, apart from brief
windows where an HSDir's consensus lags a client's: a circuit that makes
several such requests gets more HSDir work per circuit built, and a
previous hop missing from the consensus escapes DoSCircuitCreation,
which only counts client connections (tor#41339).

Add two AUTOBOOL options, HSDirOneRequestPerCircuit and
HSDirRefuseUnknownPrevHop, whose "auto" default follows the consensus
parameter of the same name, which defaults to 0. When enabled, an HSDir
closes a circuit on its second onion service directory request, and
answers a request whose previous hop is neither in its consensus nor in
its descriptor list with a 503, the check RefuseUnknownExits applies to
exit streams. As with DoSRefuseSingleHopClientRendezvous, they only
apply on public relays, and dos_should_refuse_single_hop_client() now
shares their helper.

The requests these defenses apply to are already counted on the
MetricsPort, so operators can see what enabling them would refuse.
Refused requests are counted as action "refused" in
tor_relay_hsdir_get_total and tor_relay_hsdir_post_total, so each
tunneled request is still counted once. The heartbeat shows the counts
once a defense is enabled.

Part of #41339.
